From: David J. Vandenburgh <david@vandenburgh.co>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: q6apm: probe asynchronously

The GPR bus adds the APM service while it probes, and it probes from
the ADSP's glink channel being opened, which glink does in its rx
worker. If snd-q6apm is already loaded at that point, apm_probe() runs
right there, sends APM_CMD_GET_SPF_STATE and waits up to 5 seconds for
the response. glink's rx worker is also where the commands the ADSP
needs answered first are handled (intent requests, for one), and it is
stuck in that wait, so the response never comes:

  [    4.021] Adding APR/GPR dev: gprsvc:service:2:1
  [    9.184] qcom-apm gprsvc:service:2:1: CMD timeout for [1001021] opcode

and the sound card registers 5 seconds late. When the module loads
after the ADSP is up, the probe runs from modprobe instead and the
same query is answered within 80 ms. On a Retroid Pocket 6 it went one
way or the other depending on whether the ADSP or udev won, and five
of six boots timed out.

Probe asynchronously, so the wait never happens in glink's worker.

Signed-off-by: David J. Vandenburgh <david@vandenburgh.co>
---
diff --git a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm.c b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm.c
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm.c
@@ -1045,6 +1045,14 @@
 	.driver = {
 		.name = "qcom-apm",
 		.of_match_table = of_match_ptr(apm_device_id),
+		/*
+		 * The APM service is added from glink's rx worker, while the ADSP
+		 * channel opens. A synchronous probe would wait for the APM state
+		 * response in that worker, which is also where the ADSP's intent
+		 * requests are handled, so the response could not be sent until
+		 * the wait timed out.
+		 */
+		.probe_type = PROBE_PREFER_ASYNCHRONOUS,
 	},
 };
 
